1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:02,625
Fifty thousand years ago, meeting

2
00:00:02,625 --> 00:00:04,500
another human did not always

3
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:05,775
mean meeting someone from your

4
00:00:05,775 --> 00:00:06,900
species.

5
00:00:07,350 --> 00:00:09,112
Homo sapiens shared parts of

6
00:00:09,112 --> 00:00:10,612
the prehistoric world with

7
00:00:10,612 --> 00:00:12,438
other human lineages.

8
00:00:12,438 --> 00:00:14,262
Neanderthals lived across parts

9
00:00:14,262 --> 00:00:15,838
of Europe and Asia.

10
00:00:15,838 --> 00:00:17,438
Denisovan-related groups had

11
00:00:17,438 --> 00:00:19,250
their own complicated Asian

12
00:00:19,250 --> 00:00:21,612
history. Small-bodied humans

13
00:00:21,612 --> 00:00:23,625
also lived on Flores.

14
00:00:24,025 --> 00:00:25,675
But this was not one giant

15
00:00:25,675 --> 00:00:28,075
reunion. Different groups occupied

16
00:00:28,075 --> 00:00:29,612
different places, and their

17
00:00:29,612 --> 00:00:31,475
known dates do not put everyone

18
00:00:31,475 --> 00:00:33,500
beside the same campfire.

19
00:00:33,925 --> 00:00:36,225
Today, every living human belongs

20
00:00:36,225 --> 00:00:37,912
to Homo sapiens.

21
00:00:37,912 --> 00:00:39,312
That does not make the past

22
00:00:39,312 --> 00:00:40,863
a tournament where every other

23
00:00:40,863 --> 00:00:42,700
branch was inferior.

24
00:00:43,175 --> 00:00:44,712
Some relatives disappeared

25
00:00:44,712 --> 00:00:46,750
as distinct populations.

26
00:00:46,750 --> 00:00:48,912
Some also contributed ancestry

27
00:00:48,912 --> 00:00:51,075
to later people through interbreeding.

28
00:00:51,675 --> 00:00:53,425
We are the only human species

29
00:00:53,425 --> 00:00:54,650
alive today.

30
00:00:54,650 --> 00:00:56,662
Our family history still contains

31
00:00:56,662 --> 00:00:58,450
more than one kind of human.
